Subject: [PATCH 21/24] 13-document-new-options-and-target-assumptions

---
 backend/cmm_strength_reduction.ml  |  9 ++++++++-
 backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli | 19 ++++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
 mode change 100755 => 100644 backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli

diff --git a/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.ml b/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.ml
index 0130b51d56..49265762ef 100644
--- a/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.ml
+++ b/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.ml
@@ -1027,7 +1027,14 @@ let scale_by n expr dbg =
 
 (* The scales an x86 addressing mode can apply to its index register. A wider
    step would need a shift inside the loop, which is what this pass is trying to
-   remove. *)
+   remove.
+
+   Other targets: arm64 has no scaled-index addressing mode at all; its
+   instruction selection turns [base + (index << k)] into one shifted add, for
+   any [k], followed by a load at a constant displacement. The rewritten
+   address costs that one instruction there too, so restricting the step to
+   the x86 scales only declines rewrites arm64 could also take; it does not
+   make arm64 code worse. (Not tested: no arm64 build was available.) *)
 let is_addressing_scale n = n = 0 || n = 1 || n = 2 || n = 4 || n = 8
 
 let same_base (root, invariant, bytes) (root', invariant', bytes') =
diff --git a/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli b/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli
old mode 100755
new mode 100644
index c6676a6f25..d640989eaf
--- a/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli
+++ b/backend/cmm_strength_reduction.mli
@@ -34,7 +34,9 @@
     - Strength reduction (on by default, [-no-cmm-strength-reduction]): a sum
       [b + i] of a loop-invariant [b] and an affine function [i] of a counter
       that every exit steps by a constant or resets is carried in a new loop
-      parameter, so the addition leaves the loop.
+      parameter, so the addition leaves the loop. Only sums that the backend
+      would have to materialise are carried, and only while the loop's
+      integer values fit in registers.
 
     - Interior pointers ([-no-cmm-interior-pointers]): in a loop that cannot
       reach a safepoint, the invariant part of an array address is formed once
@@ -47,7 +49,9 @@
 
     - Loop-invariant code motion ([-no-cmm-licm]): pure, total integer bindings
       whose operands are bound outside the loop are bound before it, when that
-      does not add a live range the loop cannot afford.
+      does not add a live range the loop cannot afford. In a loop that may
+      reach a safepoint, only bindings of integer operands are hoisted
+      ([-no-cmm-licm-polled] turns that off).
 
     - Counted loops ([-cmm-counted-loops], off by default): a GC-free loop whose
       termination is proved (exactly, in 64-bit wrapping arithmetic; otherwise
@@ -55,11 +59,20 @@
       back-edge poll and polls on its exits instead, and is then given the
       pointer passes even when the unit polls. [-cmm-unchecked-loops] does the
       same without the proof, at the cost of liveness only.
+      [-cmm-counted-loops-max-trips n] also requires a counted loop to end
+      within about [n] iterations, checked on entry, which bounds how long it
+      can delay a stop-the-world collection. The polled copies made for entry
+      checks may add at most twice the function's own size; a loop whose copy
+      would exceed that keeps its poll.
 
     A derived pointer is never live across a safepoint: the pointer passes
     only act where none can occur (a unit compiled without polling, or a
     counted loop), and the emitter rejects an [Addr] live at a frame
     descriptor ("bad GC root"). [-dcounted-loops] prints each counted-loop
-    decision. See the implementation for the exact conditions. *)
+    decision, and [-dcmm-loops] the [Cmm] of each function after these passes.
+
+    The cost model is tuned for amd64 (addressing-mode scales, [lea]); on
+    other 64-bit targets the rewrites are correct but untested. See the
+    implementation for the exact conditions. *)
 
 val fundecl : Cmm.fundecl -> Cmm.fundecl
